5a8d2f51071.svg)\n\nLet's sketch the points in ![](images/48/48162c9ef32c06765d5fc93f0f8d7e71457d6951bea5d902700551014a70650f.svg) which contains the joint support.\n\n![](images/a9/a9f7d560d9a8110931ceb182ad4fc14d87c55ffc4440179dfea5ce7e99a1400f.png)\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/98/9812836b2d4e19cc59460fc3482ee0a83a876d991a8ed8e0672aa93527bbfd2e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/8c/8cc3fad1e270890ee0856aaba2280c0a9fad74c9a936e214e03b65d89945b27c.svg)\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at ![](images/3d/3d6f6e7066b5c3acd802358410eeabf93c9362edeb1c199ba6a988907d6edf34.svg) and ![](images/ef/ef85d6b6f088b37f977b242067d158c487d8268faef1f8c4fb8f00455570ae22.svg)\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to add back the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/74/74527b4b8d703ba5b9fa8bfb60c5ea4c65feeed6024c3ecdbb6075007e3dc7da.svg) This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n![](images/76/76f062fddcb06564b51de85ec9dc1ba76cd2499a559ab54de725529da7c684e8.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161696 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/id #uuid "28277187-2842-5b7a-bfee-672bd4f08aca" :question/math-academy-id "e-10336" :question/problem "Suppose ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) be ![](images/45/45ba00