c117fba19710304.svg)\n\nLet's sketch the points in ![](images/48/48162c9ef32c06765d5fc93f0f8d7e71457d6951bea5d902700551014a70650f.svg) which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) where ![](images/3c/3c6bedb2ebac45a277b712f2ff734c7ace7b7cf0a65c4958eaa6e5ddd0e8f409.svg) and ![](images/3e/3e5bac1a8eeacf78dab32cf4e326af01686953a83ed4c59d43bc075fd44bd85c.svg) and a region ![](images/47/479e96bb63311ab42547a0e2ee7a7d36ec21d0a868a1e356685ab871a486c3de.svg) that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/63/63c986f8151a3b8cb30647b9613cc36d80449b16c4c28e666e13b603ee857114.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) is at least ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3e5bac1a8eeacf78dab32cf4e326af01686953a83ed4c59d43bc075fd44bd85c.svg) there are no support points satisfying ![](images/7c/7c5998784575f43e7bffccc57bcc1fab18230ae725b949e0714f4471b1f9e700.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/32/32f5eadce202f556551a333ba24d991bb052148117ab2225d46bf0109c76e626.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161697 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b97d9a7a-fa12-5d8d-8958-39625f117979" :knowledge-point/title "Further Finding Cumulative Probabilities Given Values From the Joint CDF"}] :question/id #uuid "0b991ee4-a4b3-55b4-93d1-77936f5b3067" :question/math-academy-id "e-10337" :question/problem "Suppose ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) be ![](images/45/45ba004dac2f7f8efb58a5071b600dded19307f6fbc2bc376cc780ec2054b4fa.svg) where\n\n![](images/bc/bccb7b1dec5f98fa287663b1c37d604bcb342be948cb205017e0a6aa9598efc8.svg)\n\nand the supports of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg), respectively, are\n\n![](images/6d/6d05be65088fd21af5b4b56f7ad17ff4068ea78f1f476384412a1de0aed78238.svg)\n\nFind ![](images/00/00697601602b2e721dd6f603e9b1fe88e2b06035d91f165719a5b9fc56c5aba9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF ![](images/7f/7f3705d5dab288c1c6774f437aa18dea961252d741fb302b399cb2ad18112a3a.svg) we can think of each point where ![](images/d1/d17e98e4e46a13b4d2d8f97bbcf008c339ed281a929aa3eec7ecdd7b63a9ac92.svg) as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF ![](images/24/246748e439e0fd3d76529b637ab1ac09d2308e45a826924cee283c1e9252e6ea.svg) is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/07/07fd689b67975f50da1b6b025460996fc87f21f758f5e98d34eb84c9b0e50fcd.svg) the joint support ![](images/4d/4d93e1f4798eafa4154488dfcd1445e7ca86009a6be22534670492eab898da62.svg) is a subset of\n\n![](images/b9/b9de46a0daabfc623c17c8366760c1ad801f11931fa9e274a660a25ce19977aa.svg)\n\nLet's plot the points in ![](images/48/48162c9ef32c06765d5fc93f0f8d7e71457d6951bea5d902700551014a70650f.svg) which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/91/91a0796929c7d023dd3a7110458ec2b9b3c365b8ba433c001707155c65cf4e28.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at ![](images/6b/6b867b7633ee52cd50377faf42b2bf05d25b7b7985fb452484e77c842c4869b4.svg) and top-left corner at ![](images/f9/f9312dff657ae3bae42b12f4862babdc044a9557a23a0afbd40c8efeafbe2734.svg) where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/80/80d02d152f41e88c9533d06887a925a6acd9438924b36a3c1268e04d150ea1ac.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/6b/6b867b7633ee52cd50377faf42b2bf05d25b7b7985fb452484e77c842c4869b4.svg) (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ![](images/0a/0aef6691a5c584a00fb09bbbfa667f205f5819bc0ff10de5f8e53b207de0a70c.svg) (the red region below).\n\n![](images/d0/d094730505c58c936e5d4313ca9a9fc9c6ff9fd117f591a9382ef0719f8b8df3.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n![](images/6d/6d92c2320b23f59414b763322db5af452fe7b1209c2983410e0e8959f1d1e38b.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863b10fe-f745-5566-ae64-5eb30b06c2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159669} {:answer/id #uuid "8a944662-c5dd-5606-9c9f-6709fa172b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159670} {:answer/id #uuid "a76f7163-9ca0-5f82-af73-cb015fc87e68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159671} {:answer/id #uuid "c622b1a2-e96f-5222-bc89-4198a6a07bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159672} {:answer/id #uuid "21f58f58-8d2d-5c6a-901a-783e42784a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c622b1a2-e96f-5222-bc89-4198a6a07bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159672} :answer-field/id #uuid "f03c2b00-d99f-52aa-a91b-33654f63b2f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "579f1fda-9534-54f2-9a64-f554fdec8e40" :question/math-academy-id "q-145559" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,2)+f(1,4)+f(2,2)+f(2,4) \\\\  & =0.1+0.25+0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.55.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbc5089-2da3-5298-974d-ece99d9afcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480822} {:answer/id #uuid "4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480873} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb4c7f7-e3ac-5e28-8130-565a87c9fd9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480909} {:answer/id #uuid "63bbbc94-2421-5b0d-8245-dc342d764984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480923} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2b7f5a-d6cd-5bd8-9453-67d08d568d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4703c062-7fb3-5337-a7b8-1155f59f3774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480873} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e92015-0288-5022-836f-fa11f4607dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481241}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40beaebe-e39b-5263-8664-2ca0bd457e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-146338" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 0.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,0) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 0}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,0)+f(2,0) \\\\  & =0.35+0.25 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d9c2a22-1745-5f11-90c5-b61178746ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519038} {:answer/id #uuid "68316cdc-7e2e-5c8c-b2c0-9fdb5db4e265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519144} {:answer/id #uuid "8beedfa2-a23b-56ef-9e81-c3c00d1c853c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519218} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ff47ca-4ea7-5a98-9758-b8c161eaa30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519284} {:answer/id #uuid "badb182a-b77f-549d-99b6-8f6630a12a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68316cdc-7e2e-5c8c-b2c0-9fdb5db4e265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519144} :answer-field/id #uuid "70912616-2902-589e-b985-cc6b891d3822" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "097fc2ae-2fef-58ac-bba4-f69c8c04dea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-146341" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 3.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,3) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3)+f(2,1)+f(2,2)+f(2,3) \\\\  & =0.1+0.2+0.2+0.15+0.1+0.1 \\\\  & =0.85.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480779} {:answer/id #uuid "59fa7bbf-b27b-58e4-a896-8340b8f2bdb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480903} {:answer/id #uuid "5cff4ee6-31ec-5c09-907b-5027d5a24e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480911} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab89f8e-8046-55ef-a939-0ea4407202fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480964} {:answer/id #uuid "b16c880f-0f86-582b-ae1f-223c2802503d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481073}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1657cbc2-f103-5ad9-8c0b-3b96b07759b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480779} :answer-field/id #uuid "960a006f-092b-5cc0-ae1b-106bba927b26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481293}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c34fa369-1de0-5740-997b-e34e018ecb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-146389" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & =P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 2,Y\\le 4.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.15$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(2,4) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 2}{\\sum }_{y\\le 4}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(1,1)+f(1,2)+f(1,3) \\\\  & =0.2+0.3+0.1 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162124 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3c48f82-c3ed-5014-b1f5-cd9e8de542f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Joint CDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07cfc785-fa96-5e22-b467-263332177c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519003} {:answer/id #uuid "3ae4d64b-01dc-56eb-9afe-2fc8eba19d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519082} {:answer/id #uuid "bb134a2b-094e-5864-a589-8e05c37b430c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519298} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f045a-855b-58ea-9cc2-fe6f72264ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519310} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e63b2b-06b8-588f-bae2-b8c4173b063c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c11f045a-855b-58ea-9cc2-fe6f72264ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519310} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c4d7f13-80f9-5cf8-8777-77cdf1cbaa54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9adae9aa-2d5a-51d9-80a6-fbe056bdc031" :question/math-academy-id "q-146394" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nBy definition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & =P(X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by highlighting all cells in the table that correspond to $X\\le 0,Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=-1$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=-1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.1$ |\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0,\\frac{3}{2}) & ={\\sum }_{x\\le 0}{\\sum }_{y\\le 3/2}f(x,y) \\\\  & =f(-1,-1)+f(-1,0)+f(-1,1) \\\\  & =0.15+0.25+0.05 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba4c4de-57ee-5011-8a90-f13d0828a75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519010} {:answer/id #uuid "1adee21a-d35b-5748-b6a5-c4ca64b76fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519033} {:answer/id #uuid "7caa5188-b509-5b3c-83ea-c26692b3a05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x}+\\frac{3}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519189} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9fe426-9283-5444-8aef-eed1d7f5ef25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519217} {:answer/id #uuid "d8f67f35-fb6d-5689-a266-7081cbdaa5cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b9fe426-9283-5444-8aef-eed1d7f5ef25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519217} :answer-field/id #uuid "be61e717-c5ed-51d0-853e-521eeca3a460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "741ee6eb-9658-524e-8fcb-9b428519f04d" :question/math-academy-id "q-146408" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,2,4,6\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>6,y>3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,2,4,6\\}\\times \\{0,1,3\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>6$ and $y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/63/6340ca35646830558631c7fe523698a201c5acf26d1360910ab207c30f92e41f.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $6$ and $x>6,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $3$ and $y>3,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480919} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aa2cdd-73fe-5eb0-a310-26b21184ef73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481080} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c3a3c7-6f3b-5337-8e2c-82b7f32fd2ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481133} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c1b40c-90e0-5d0c-8b12-94ea0606a002" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{y}-\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481214} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7c9232-17f6-5028-afcd-f94db070b33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "616c295b-4960-58a5-ae57-474d899990b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480919} :answer-field/id #uuid "c47eb18a-bfe5-5e49-bb9e-a97fe694f936" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5555465c-a779-5eab-9f3a-3128b4e5cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-146409" :question/problem "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{0,1\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,y>6.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{0,1\\}\\times \\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1$ and $y>6,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/64/644db930cdb0d683a0bcae61b2596dbaeaa4c46df45d8347053c742d2ded3af9.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at most $1$ and $x>1,$ every possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Similarly, every value in the support of $Y$ is at most $6$ and $y>6,$ so every possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, the region $Δ$ contains the entire joint support, and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162130 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b8dd2d-ecaa-58bf-baa3-72dc8f37523f" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Joint CDF Geometrically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6348da3e-92c0-531f-880b-2b60144620aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519137} {:answer/id #uuid "63931c2b-dbd9-5238-a4ce-8afa3c114b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519138} {:answer/id #uuid "9d6c637a-7279-5005-b641-b00576d1fb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519250} {:answer/id #uuid "d59dea64-e112-532f-be9e-3afb325fc308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/mat